{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788486774-Nz0Zej3QGLTkFX7y93Tq","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the structure. This is aiogram, and:\n\n- `Dispatcher.start_polling()` has `handle_as_tasks: bool = True` and passes it to `_polling()`.\n- `_polling()` creates a task per update via `asyncio.create_task` when `handle_as_tasks=True`, tracking them in `self._handle_update_tasks`.\n- `run_polling()` wraps `start_polling()`.\n\nI need to add a `tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None` parameter to both `start_polling()` and `run_polling()`, thread it through to `_polling()`, and create a semaphore when the limit is set and `handle_as_tasks=True`.\n\nLet me check the actual aiogram upstream implementation for reference. In aiogram 3.x, there's a real feature — PR #1662 or similar \"Add tasks_concurrency_limit to polling\" — actually I recall aiogram added this in version 3.16 or so. Let me check the changelog structure to see how changes are documented.\n\nLet me look at:\n1. CHANGES.rst / CHANGES directory for changelog format\n2. Tests for dispatcher polling\n3. How the version is set\n\nActually let me check the actual upstream aiogram implementation. In aiogram 3.20 (or 3.16?), there's this in `_polling`:\n\n```python\nasync def _polling(\n    self,\n    bot: Bot,\n    polling_timeout: int = 30,\n    handle_as_tasks: bool = True,\n    backoff_config: BackoffConfig = DEFAULT_BACKOFF_CONFIG,\n    allowed_updates: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n    tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None,\n    **kwargs: Any,\n) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Internal polling process\n\n    :param bot:\n    :param kwargs:\n    :return:\n    \"\"\"\n    user: User = await bot.me()\n    loggers.dispatcher.info(\n        \"Run polling for bot @%s id=%d - %r\", user.username, bot.id, user.full_name\n    )\n    try:\n        semaphore = (\n            asyncio.Semaphore(tasks_concurrency_limit) if tasks_concurrency_limit else None\n        )\n        async for update in self._listen_updates(\n            bot,\n            polling_timeout=polling_timeout,\n            backoff_config=backoff_config,\n            allowed_updates=allowed_updates,\n        ):\n            handle_update = self._process_update(bot=bot, update=update, **kwargs)\n            if handle_as_tasks:\n                if semaphore:\n                    # Limit the number of concurrent tasks\n                    await semaphore.acquire()\n                handle_update_task = asyncio.create_task(\n                    self._process_update_with_semaphore(...)  # something like this\n                )\n                ...\n```\n\nLet me recall the actual upstream aiogram commit. The feature is from aiogram PR #1649 \"Added ability to limit the number of concurrently processed updates in polling mode\" — changelog entry something like:\n\n```\nFixed memory leak (Out of Memory) while processing a large queue of updates in polling mode with :code:`handle_as_tasks=True` by adding the new argument :param:`tasks_concurrency_limit` to the :meth:`aiogram.Dispatcher.start_polling` and :meth:`aiogram.Dispatcher.run_polling` methods...\n```\n\nActually the real upstream aiogram 3.16.0 changelog says:\n\n\"Fixed possible OOM (Out of Memory) error while polling with a large queue of updates and handle_as_tasks=True...\"\n\nLet me look at the actual implementation in aiogram 3.16+:\n\n```python\n    async def _polling(\n        self,\n        bot: Bot,\n        polling_timeout: int = 30,\n        handle_as_tasks: bool = True,\n        backoff_config: BackoffConfig = DEFAULT_BACKOFF_CONFIG,\n        allowed_updates: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n        tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None,\n        **kwargs: Any,\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Internal polling process\n\n        :param bot:\n        :param kwargs:\n        :return:\n        \"\"\"\n        user: User = await bot.me()\n        logger