{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788486895-Ao1SYZ6XTuGanvRZuc9z","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Everything checks out:\n- `bugfix` is a valid towncrier type, so `CHANGES/1658.bugfix.rst` will be picked up.\n- All three methods accept `tasks_concurrency_limit` with default `None`.\n- `_process_with_semaphore` exists.\n\nNow let me do an end-to-end functional verification that the semaphore actually limits concurrency and releases on both success and failure — a behavior test beyond the unit tests. Let me write a quick script that:\n1. Uses `start_polling` with `tasks_concurrency_limit=2`, slow handlers, and verifies max concurrency ≤ 2 and all updates processed.\n2. Verifies handlers raising exceptions still release the semaphore slot.\n3. Verifies no-limit behavior unchanged.\n\nI'll simulate with a real Dispatcher, patching `_listen_updates` to yield a bunch of updates and using real handlers with delays. Use the MockedBot from the test infra, or just a stub bot? `start_polling` needs `bot.me()`. Easier to reuse the test fixtures approach — I'll write a small standalone script using MockedBot from aiogram's test utils (available in the repo's tests conftest, importable as `tests.test_dispatcher.mocked_bot`? Let me check where MockedBot is defined).","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Together","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f1101d0-3721-48e8-b2bc-05484b9b7dc0","uuid":"fc129799-fbba-4ae6-ab18-3c70e522cf2f","timestamp":"2026-09-04T01:54:58.700Z","request_id":"gen-1788486895-Ao1SYZ6XTuGanvRZuc9z"}
